<p><img decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/2026-02-22-the-erdos-faber-lovasz-conjecture.png" alt="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erd%C5%91s%E2%80%93Faber%E2%80%93Lov%C3%A1sz_conjecture.svg" width="600" height="640" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-72742" srcset="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/2026-02-22-the-erdos-faber-lovasz-conjecture.png 600w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/2026-02-22-the-erdos-faber-lovasz-conjecture-300x320.png 300w" sizes="(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 600px" /></p>
<p>This figure contains four &#8220;cliques&#8221; of four points each, with each of the four points in each clique connected to each of the others, and each pair of cliques intersecting at a single point. Four colors suffice to color all the points so that no two linked points share a color.</p>
<p>Is this always possible? If <em>k</em> cliques, each containing <em>k</em> points, are arranged in similar fashion, can the result always be colored properly with <em>k</em> colors? In 2021, half a century after Paul Erd&#337;s first posed the question, Dong Yeap Kang and his colleagues proved that, for sufficiently large <em>k</em>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.04698">the conjecture is true</a>.</p>
